+| A4 | This window attribution sentence repeats one documentary quotation without new evidence. | S903 | "Effects marked as _dynamic_ are re-evaluated whenever the matching property of the window changes. For instance, if a rule changes the `border_color`" |
+| A5 | This window attribution sentence repeats one documentary quotation without new evidence. | S901 | "Effects marked as _dynamic_ are re-evaluated whenever the matching property of the window changes. For instance, if a rule changes the `border_color`" |
+| A6 | This window attribution sentence repeats one documentary quotation without new evidence. | S903 | "Effects marked as _dynamic_ are re-evaluated whenever the matching property of the window changes. For instance, if a rule changes the `border_color`" |
+| A7 | This attribution sentence is assembled from disconnected reversed source code fragments. | S902 | "const auto PROP = vars[2]; return "not enough args";" |
+| A8 | This window attribution sentence exceeds the frozen twenty five word quotation limit. | S903 | "Effects marked as _dynamic_ are re-evaluated whenever the matching property of the window changes. For instance, if a rule changes the `border_color` when a window is floating, the color reverts" |
+
+A1–A6 repeat the same 22-word normalized quote under six claim IDs and alternating source IDs. The source contains a newline after `changes.`; each row flattens it, and A2 also doubles spaces. A7 joins two non-adjacent source fragments in reverse source order, jointly testing splicing and word-order manipulation. A8 is an exact whitespace-collapsed 30-word source span. Combining the splice and order manipulation keeps the full fixture at exactly eight claim rows.
